{"title":"Subjectwise Test-2 Part Syllabus GATE 2025 EC Control Systems","fileName":"035_Subjectwise Test-2 Part Syllabus GATE 2025 EC Control Systems.html","branch":"ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING","series":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series Electronics Engineering","provider":"MADE EASY","duration":5400,"questions":[{"qnum":1,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the open loop transfer function of a feedback control system,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/de5a7661ed1e721ee8bcab0966ab7a23bdbbd5d5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n where input to the system is (1 + 2t)2u(t). Then which of the following results are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Steady state error due to step input is zero.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Steady state error due to velocity input is 1.379.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Steady state error due to acceleration input is zero.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Velocity error constant is zero.</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (a, b)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/e9a2f77c8b1cd577faf4779e92e6049ca41fc166.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":2,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the state space description of an LTI system as shown below:</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/5c4556defe4f427fe41d115bb0951fc2a9f94c6d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n If the input <img src=\"/media/76bba8c4bc5bc8cdb8d8015955cc7db003987b10.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> = sin ωt; ω > 0, then the value of ω for which the steady-state output of the system will be zero is _______ rad/sec.</p>\n<p>\n (Round off to two decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"1.2","high":"1.5"},"solution":"<p>\n 1.41 (1.20 - 1.50)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/66e69e9838bc511d4ce5ae3103f8d25baaec88aa.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/0fc9fcdcc2f27ed21128682fc40446fb84925a03.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":3,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider a state model for a system characterized by the differential equation</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/55f7346e4221d9905e5849183afa5e9cabf45dbc.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n Then which of the following block diagram correctly matches to the given state model?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/739e544558c5b43c4f1e1f9d189ab661236e3959.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/3dd7b18280bf28b910a1b45ba49c8179912c1171.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/e1d237162570fe198897fdf87ec5e709b5d79a1c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/e234a684af30e775fd9f14aa06fee678bbcddce2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (a)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/b1de8825f2776b778282276e50c85055ceef7b23.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":4,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the block diagram of a system shown below:</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/d528942cf06c0551e7ecbe6548d554ec591b4973.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n The given system is said to be</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> a minimum phase system</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> an unstable system</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> a stable system</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> a marginally stable system</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (b)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/6e28cbff870c568e7a54b388060063280ebc344d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":5,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n For the system shown below,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/b2b619b1aed22d98576fba32d3620939769e241b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n The value of <em>K</em> that results in critical damping is ______. (upto two decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"0.3","high":"0.5"},"solution":"<p>\n 0.417 (0.30 - 0.50)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/f54f798e124eca03e6a7a6b07d10dffbaa67cda1.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":6,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the root locus diagram for a unity feedback system with open-loop transfer function, <img src=\"/media/595b276a954688edda72542adbc58f7f7e174294.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> The intersection of the asymptotes on the real axis occurs at</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> –1.2</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> –0.85</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> –0.75</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> –1.05</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (c)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/417dafa38d612a4ed1c89e5d4075b8d7b54f6c54.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":7,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Open loop transfer function of a feedback system is <em>G</em>(<em>s</em>)<em>H</em>(<em>s</em>) = <img src=\"/media/a5a84fd79efc70dd9b007b79e74ee44da4849903.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> Phase margin of system is _________ degree. (upto two decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"62.16","high":"65.13"},"solution":"<p>\n 64.06 (62.16 - 65.13)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/62931a91558ea5148b96ea07be4190f2df0aecf7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":8,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the system shown below :</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/cda35fbf9e81523fc384ba6686788ac7a144d02e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n The steady state offset due to D(s) will be zero for</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <em>K</em> = 0</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n <em>K</em> = 0.3</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n <em>K</em> = 2</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n <em>K</em> = ∞</p></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (d)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/4bbbfa023dc7d55d446d3927be6a95cbe4e8ca8b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":9,"type":"MSQ","correct":"B,C","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the polar plot of two systems as shown below,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/6a976d08a3da878f9254af4767378ae66f969122.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n Then, which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <em>A</em> is unstable</p>\n</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n <em>A</em> is stable</p>\n</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n <em>B</em> is marginally stable</p>\n</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n <em>B</em> is unstable</p>\n</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (b, c)<br />\n As <em>A</em> lies to the right of (&ndash;1, 0), the Nyquist pole doesn&#39;t encircle (&ndash;1, 0) point, hence system <em>A</em> is stable and <em>B</em> cuts the real axial at (&ndash;1, 0), therefore it is marginally stable.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":10,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n The open loop pole zero plot of a negative feedback system is shown below:</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/de335998d569ffc14130b4265e3117d5155baba8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n The appropriate root locus plot of this system is given by</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/5ad4c415e233b8d133d798cc7101c0f210fefee9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/84e741c5c61de7e287c806c0218feffd940373a7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/8ad1be3888f32aafbfab1419c74062db9f2b2395.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/fc5ff4d5d4a03915d96c094d67fef023e8b9403e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (a)<br />\n Root locus always starts from open loop poles and terminates at open loop zeros. Also, a point on the real axis lies on the root locus if the total number of poles and zeros to the right of this point is odd. Thus, only option (a) satisfies.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":11,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n The Bode magnitude plot of a lag compensator is given in the figure below:</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/8a4fbc64415dde341748e342d805def1099e7e08.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n The angular frequency at which the phase lag provided by this compensator will be maximum is ________ rad/sec. (upto two decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"3.6","high":"3.9"},"solution":"<p>\n 3.74 (3.60 - 3.90)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/40c34374be546a3948be06de851df5aacc451a13.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":12,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the compensator network shown in the figure below:</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/4ec7fb25dcd61c30c595d232efed729737a74cad.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n The maximum phase difference between the input and output voltage of the network is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/37b9c4f5c27811333689759c34fc4fc2070d5cf0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/78e4ab95be56f68a176d593900afc0c087567857.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/3491fc86649dcafaec03c2d03a74289cf5fce1d7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/a66d7ec0372f42176746edd2b22ca68b18ba36bb.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (b)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/099321158bf2712e889709374e7ffa58ca142b9a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/7f87021a998b017e5cda0a1eabc813bd2bfbeea1.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":13,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n Which of the following transfer function corresponds to the Bode magnitude plot shown below?</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/4417452aa183bff66d717e007b8f92bd47e7c77b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/8488712a3a8fe4349bcae36343a95ed68ed0cc94.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/c2982807a544b3582fb7695ee33fb6ee174c4a22.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/36be3676685327ca9a82d513e6327101a547e76c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/dd819130df8c55b54c955b1938079e80bbae258d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (d)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/28c21c628fa5d3eb549b3e3a6d0831af1f6f8421.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":14,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n The response of a system shown in figure (a), to an input of <em>r</em>(<em>t</em>) = 5u(<em>t</em>), is shown in figure (b).<br />\n The time constant <img src=\"/media/2bbf75cdd77b04cf7029afac4db81b9afe1e0692.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> of the system is equal to</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/b9cb130161e0942a37d8d358df8284837a447a6a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 0.35 msec</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0.29 msec</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 0.19 msec</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 0.083 msec</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (c)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/d30b05492c08117f8954e162d685c71646a8bbcf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":15,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n The state model of a system is given as,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/81fa79651b9903935503aa2ddb229e60f3de35d7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n For unit step input, the system response is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> undamped</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> underdamped</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> critically damped</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> overdamped</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (d)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/11e0c8e4e20052dce9001931db5855f8ae7bb660.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/c43f2b9a53240503e1e6f031dc31a029bd39dcf8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":16,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n The characteristic equation of a negative feedback control system is given by,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/7e1d632490b4ead95340a9d387cf4620041804b8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n The value of resonant frequency ω<em><sub>r</sub></em> of this system is _______ rad/sec. (upto two decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"5.55","high":"5.75"},"solution":"<p>\n 5.66 (5.55 - 5.75)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/130ba7cfb6fbe5bcb2a71dcf9a180c6ae40b37d7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":17,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n The characteristic equation of a unity feedback control system is given by,<br />\n <em>s</em><sup>6</sup> + 2<em>s</em><sup>5</sup> + 8<em>s</em><sup>4</sup> + 12<em>s</em><sup>3</sup> + 20<em>s</em><sup>2</sup> + 16s + 16 = 0<br />\n The given system is said to be</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> stable</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> marginally stable</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> conditionally stable</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> unstable</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (b)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/654600ddff4c4a4dd4eab58bc48191e6331e8012.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/50c0125e9e0a9559686a4d2b1529f9f0d3a52f70.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":18,"type":"NAT","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Magnitude plot of a unity negative feedback system is shown in figure</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/fa042b033d9d3ad176b7623f25aa25e9bb0d7157.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n Steady state error for unit ramp input is ________ × 10<sup>–3</sup>. (Give answer upto three decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"1.031","high":"1.512"},"solution":"<p>\n 1.265 (1.031 - 1.512)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/0394909b694a08c5df1f6be869502255a2571fb4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":19,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n The open loop transfer function of a unity negative feedback control system is given by</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/8c1649427aeace34c400cb40c103a33e2544639a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n If <img src=\"/media/ab5a542ef31f537db1b07d00946cd59da5365011.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> satisfies the condition for gain cross-over frequency, then the gain margin and the phase margin of the system are respectively</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> GM = 0 dB and PM = 180°</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> GM = 0 dB and PM = 0°</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> GM = ∞ and PM = 0°</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> GM = ∞ and PM = 180°</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (b)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/b0fff27346e6480464d0bbc2dd47b76737e0b78a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":20,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Polar plot of a lead compensator is given by</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/1f78e0a735cb7ee2a8e13ca42b96daec527974fd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n Maximum phase lead introduced by the compensator is _______ degree.</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"27","high":"33"},"solution":"<p>\n 30 (27 - 33)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/84e56a10e3c3bf66abff68bb941ccb1770f87d5a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/60d12956d54cf611f66c5a1b44889ce732f009aa.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":21,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n A unity negative feedback control system has uncompensated open loop transfer function,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/aa9e7dbfaa1d84f2680e3897d579aea61dbb2c01.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n If it is required that the steady state error of the system for unit ramp excitation should be less than 0.05, then which one of the following compensators should be connected in cascade with <em>G</em>(<em>s</em>), to achieve the desired error performance?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/bceee3cc6dd20d934943e91817968c3ca12555d9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<d